> Integrating with runtime types
__doc_meta__: true
description: 'Integrating with runtime types, eg pydantic'
<

Sometimes it's useful to define richer configuration types than are supported
by the underlying backend. In this case, you can define a type coercer,
which implements the ``configatron.types.TypeCoercer`` protocol -- a callable
that receives a ``config_cls`` and ``value`` as arguments, and returns an
instance of the config class after any type coercion is performed.

This allows application developers to use (for example) pydantic, without
introducing a dependency on pydantic within the libraries they import that
use configatron for configuration.

For example:

>
__embed__: 'code/python'
    from typing import Any

    from pydantic import TypeAdapter


    def my_coercer[T](config_cls: type[T], value: Any) -> T:
        # Note: for better performance, you would want to cache the created
        # type adapter, so that subsequence coercion calls can reuse it
        adapter = TypeAdapter(config_cls)
        return adapter.validate_python(value)


    # Later, when creating your config manager...
    cfg_manager = CfgManager(
        MyConfigImpl,
        backends=...,
        type_coercer=my_coercer)


This will use the pydantic type adapter to coerce the types received from the
backend into the types you declared in the config implementation's
``ext_dataclass``, just as you'd expect. For example, using the config schema
defined in the quickstart, and the memory backend:

>
__embed__: 'code/python'
    from configatron.prebaked.backends.memory import MemoryBackend
    from configatron.prebaked.backends.memory import MemoryBackendKey
    test_backend = MemoryBackend({
        MemoryBackendKey('my.app', 'host', 'host'): 'localhost',
        # NOTE THAT THE PORT NUMBER IS A STRING!
        MemoryBackendKey('my.app', 'port', 'port'): '7007',
        MemoryBackendKey('my.app', 'app_secret', 'COOKIE_SECRET'): 'super secret'})

    cfg_manager = CfgManager(
        MyConfigImpl,
        backends={'env_vars': test_backend},
        type_coercer=my_coercer)
    cfg_manager.load_once_sync()

    # Note that the port has been coerced into an integer!
    assert (~MyConfigImpl).port == 7007
